You extract two kinds of facts from a piece of narrative text, for a graph database. {{ universe }} universe.
Use ONLY what the text says. Do not use outside knowledge of the series.

=== 1. ACTION: what a character/group does, wants, can, or must do ===

For every clause where someone does, wants, is able to, or has to do something to/toward someone or
something, output one action:

- "subject": who acts. Resolve pronouns and aliases to the CANONICAL name from the roster below. A generic
  plural or collective noun ("the students", "the crowd", "everyone") is never output as-is: resolve it to
  the specific group from the roster if the text makes clear which group, otherwise use the literal word
  "people". A bare generic noun is never allowed as the final subject.
- "subject_type": "character", "group", or "people" (matching how you resolved subject above).
- "action": ONE word, base form, the GENERALIZED outcome of what happened -- neutral to this universe's
  setting and mechanics. Keep the outcome, drop the method: stab / blast / punch -> attack; warp -> move;
  "developed an interest in" -> like; defeated -> defeat; killed -> kill (not attack -- killing is a
  different outcome from attacking).
- "action_raw": the literal verb/verb phrase from the text, unedited, for auditing "action" against.
- "manner": HOW it was done, one or two words (cruelly, reluctantly, playfully, with regret, secretly).
  Empty string if the text does not say.
- "target": who/what it is done to. A character, a specific group, "people" (same collective-noun rule as
  subject), or, if it is a thing/idea rather than an agent, a short concept noun phrase. Never a clause or a
  verb -- a noun only.
- "target_type": "character", "group", "people", or "concept".
- "complement": a THIRD participant or the outcome/role named beyond subject and target, if the sentence has
  one (e.g. "X wanted Y to become a musician" -> target "Y", complement "musician"). Empty string if there
  is none. Never a clause -- a short noun phrase only.
- "modality": "did" (happened or stated as true), "wants" (a stated desire/intent), "can" (a stated
  ability), "must" (a stated obligation/need).
- "negated": true only if the text explicitly denies the action ("did not help", "refused to"). Never true
  just because the action is absent from the text.
- "source_sentence": the exact sentence/clause this comes from, copied word for word.

=== 2. DESCRIBED_AS: a character directly described as having a trait ===

Only when the text directly states a trait ("she is intelligent", "known for being kind") -- most often in
a Personality-type section. Never infer a trait from an action; only extract it when the text states it as
a description.

- "character": who is being described (canonical name).
- "trait": ONE word, a base-form adjective (intelligent, kind, reckless, loyal -- not "quick to anger",
  pick the single adjective closest to it, e.g. "irritable").
- "said_by": "narrator" if the text states it as fact, or the canonical name of the character whose opinion
  it is if the text attributes the description to someone's judgement or speech.
- "negated": true only if the text explicitly denies the trait.
- "source_sentence": copied word for word.

